narrative:
Apothecary Nella Vey corrects your use of the word 'contagion' before answering. Her own cough is hidden behind a sleeve.

@Nella: It is recurrent, not new. The grammar matters. So does the ruin cluster where it always begins.

She gives you directions with the precision of someone afraid to be asked to come along.

Rumors outrun wagons. Healer Yssa Coombe has turned a stable into a clinic, while Quarantine-Warden Bettina Grask counts bottles as if numbers might multiply under pressure. Fen, the gravedigger's boy, waits nearby with mud on his boots.

@Yssa: I need hands, clean water, and people who do not lie about being ill. I will accept two.
@Fen: My mum says don't sell your coat. She sold hers. That was before winter.

Elder Pella dies before the bells finish their first warning. The clinic fills in a day. Hendrik's cough has become blood on a cloth he tries to hide. Yssa does not soften the truth.

@Yssa: We have one prepared dose that might carry him through. There will be other people who need it.

Outside, Corvin Ashby tells a frightened crowd that the sickness has chosen them for a reason.

At dawn, the fever simply stops. Nella Vey is more frightened by this than she was by the bodies.

@Nella: Diseases do not forgive. They finish, or they change. This did neither.

No one has an answer worth carrying home.
